<figure class="progress" data-r20-progress aria-labelledby="r20-progress-title">
 <figcaption id="r20-progress-title"><span>Figure 1</span> The copy has the bytes before its reader has the order</figcaption>
 <p class="key">Chosen log positions: <b>0</b> before purchase · <b>1</b> purchase changes · <b>2</b> commit record</p>
 <div class="rows"><div><b>Primary durable log</b><div class="rail" data-primary data-live>0</div></div><div><b>Replica received</b><div class="rail" data-received data-live>0</div></div><div><b>Replica durable log</b><div class="rail" data-durable data-live>0</div></div><div><b>Replica replayed</b><div class="rail" data-replayed data-live>0</div></div></div>
 <div class="answer"><b>Fresh replica query for 103</b><p data-answer data-live>No order 103</p></div>
 <p data-explanation data-live aria-live="polite">Both copies start before Mina's purchase. The replica can answer queries from its earlier consistent state.</p>
 <div class="controls" role="group" aria-label="Replication progress steps"><button type="button" data-play>Play</button><button type="button" data-back>Back</button><button type="button" data-next>Next</button><button type="button" data-reset>Reset</button><span data-step>1 / 5</span><div data-hold><span></span></div></div>
 <details><summary>All progress states as text</summary><ol><li>All positions are 0; no purchase103.</li><li>The primary durably commits through position2 and can return success. Replica positions remain0.</li><li>The replica receives through2. That says bytes arrived, not that its storage or queries reached2.</li><li>The replica flushes through position 2. The commit can survive a replica process crash, provided its storage honours the flush contract. Replay remains at 0, so fresh queries still lack order 103.</li><li>Replay reaches2. A new snapshot can see103, its two lines and P3stock10/P7stock0. A previously retained old snapshot need not see it.</li></ol></details>
 <p class="caption">Conceptual positions, not PostgreSQL LSN values or measured delays. Separate receive and flush steps explain the promise; the native check observes PostgreSQL's durable receive position while replay is deliberately paused.</p>
</figure>
